{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs realized prices, especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift (≈ -1 to -3 €/t on average; larger at longer horizons).",
    "Downtrend underreaction/lag: when the last 2–4 weeks show a clear decline, the model declines too slowly and predicts rebounds that do not materialize. Increase negative drift proportional to recent slope (e.g., subtract an extra 0.1–0.2 €/t per day for each -1 €/t/week recent trend).",
    "False mean-reversion: forecasts repeatedly revert upward toward ~70–74 even when the market stays weak (~61–67). Suppress mean-reversion signals when price is below its 20–30d moving average and the MA is falling; anchor forecasts closer to the moving average minus a small discount.",
    "Horizon error grows with time: overestimation increases from ~day 1–5 (small) to ~day 10–30 (large). Use a horizon-dependent correction ramp (e.g., subtract 0.5 €/t by day 5, 1.5 €/t by day 10, 3–6 €/t by day 30 in strong selloffs).",
    "Overshoot after short-term upticks/spikes: after brief rallies in history, the model extrapolates too much upside and misses subsequent fade. Cap upside projections and apply faster decay back toward the prevailing trend (reduce forecasted rebound amplitude by ~30–50%)."
  ]
}
